[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked known: one issue, one fix queued. The reports merged into it
describe three different problems. One fix for the parser leaves two of them open.

<Callout kind="warn">
Not one issue. Three tickets, one per problem. Each needs its own fix and its own owner.
</Callout>

## What was merged

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} issues={{ parser: `${repo}/1`, hint: `${repo}/2`, 'one-day': `${repo}/3` }} />

The counts come from the same data and the same rules as the desk's own table below: one report per
id, cause by tenant (`causes.ts`), and the one-day moves the desk already listed in `desk-notes.json`.

## The three problems

### `parser`: day and month swapped

The shared parser reads every date month first. Every tenant on file writes the day first, so
`7 April` goes out as `4 July`. Reports come in by app, email and form from every tenant.
Fixing the parser fixes these, and only these.

### `hint`: the booking-v3 form asks for the wrong order

On tenants running booking-v3, the form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People type
what the hint tells them, month first, and the form reads it the tenant's way, day first. The
date comes out swapped just like `parser`, which is why it was merged. But a parser fix does not
touch it: the form hint has to match the tenant's locale. Only form reports from booking-v3 tenants
land here.

### `one-day`: the invite lands one day off

These are not swaps. "Tuesday 12 May" arrives as "Monday 11 May"; the same meeting shows Tuesday
in Lisbon and Monday in Toronto; an invite arrives before its booking was made. That is a time
zone shift, not a date format. The desk noticed them (`desk-notes.json`) but `CauseTable` files
them under `parser` by tenant, so they would stay broken after the parser fix.

## How they got merged

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on subject line alone, and
[triage](/kb/triage/dedupe) says "when unsure, merge". All three problems produce the same
subjects ("Wrong day on the invite", "Month and day swapped?"), so they merged into one ticket.
Intake's tag is no better: it guesses from the reporter's browser, not the tenant, and disagrees
with the cause on many reports (see "tagged as" above).

Merging on subject needs a new decision record that replaces 0001. Until then: before merging into
a big ticket, check the cause, not the subject.

